 and agricultural wastewater (ETJ). Historically ~80% of Guadalajara wastewater untreated; collectors discharged into the barranca from >500 m height. Urban streams Atemajac/San Juan de Dios, Rio Blanco and Arroyo de Osorio enter the canyon carrying wastewater (see urban_streams). Former Matatlan landfill (ETJ lists rehabilitation of 'vertedero de Matatlan'); waste/recycling sites in the buffer. Urbanization at the rim, pig farms, agricultural burning, grazing (ETJ management actions).\"],[\"Arcediano dam\",\"Presa de Arcediano: planned on the Santiago just below the Verde confluence to supply Guadalajara; announced officially in 2003; Arcediano village displaced and suspension bridge dismantled; roughly 700 million pesos spent; suspended/cancelled in late 2009 (cost, and contamination of the source water) - described as discarded by CONAGUA in 2009; state said it could be resumed later.\"]]","src":"[\"https://transparencia.info.jalisco.gob.mx/node/13081\",\"https://riosantiago.jalisco.gob.mx/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/formacion_natural_estatal_barraca_de_los_rios_santiago_y_verde.pdf\",\"https://fiprodefo.jalisco.gob.mx/sites/fiprodefo.jalisco.gob.mx/files/anexo_8_tabla_mapa_componente_ii_anps.pdf\",\"https://riosantiago.jalisco.gob.mx/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/formacion_natural_estatal_barraca_de_los_rios_santiago_y_verde.pdf\",\"https://www.openstreetmap.org/\",\"https://riosantiago.jalisco.gob.mx/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/formacion_natural_estatal_barraca_de_los_rios_santiago_y_verde.pdf\"]"}},{"type":"Feature","geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-103.31953,20.43148],[-103.31835,20.43429],[-103.31721,20.43573],[-103.3155,20.43673],[-103.31446,20.43684],[-103.31462,20.43603],[-103.31425,20.43602],[-103.31419,20.43555],[-103.31309,20.43585],[-103.31398,20.4354],[-103.31355,20.43423],[-103.31407,20.43305],[-103.31258,20.43201],[-103.31227,20.43067],[-103.3118,20.43034],[-103.30964,20.42655],[-103.3013,20.42398],[-103.29872,20.42182],[-103.29571,20.42011